The generated syscall tables, one pair of files per platform and CPU (`linux_x86_64.hpp` and
`.cpp`), written by `tooling/gen_syscalls.py` (`make syscalls`) on the machine they describe. Never
edit them by hand: regenerate.

The header defines `ca::syscall::number::<name>` and `ca::syscall::<name>(args...)` for every syscall
the running kernel implements, read from the kernel's build headers and configuration, so
syscalls configured out of that kernel are left out. Each function takes exactly the kernel's
parameters, named as in its prototype; syscalls that never return are `[[noreturn]]`. The `.cpp`
checks every number against `<sys/syscall.h>` when the library builds.

`../syscalls.hpp` includes the table of the build target among those present, and defines
`has_table`. A target without a table still has `ca::syscall::invoke`, and `ca::sys` falls back
to libc there.

```c++
#include "ca/syscall.hpp"

static_assert(!ca::syscall::has_table || ca::syscall::number::read == 0);
static_assert(!ca::syscall::has_table || ca::syscall::number::write == 1);
```
